Same business call ("place order") expressed three ways. The right protocol depends on **coupling tolerance**, not preference.

```java
// ====== 1) REST: simple, universal, synchronous ======
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
public class OrderController {
    private final InventoryClient inventory;            // RestTemplate / WebClient
    @PostMapping
    public OrderDto place(@RequestBody OrderRequest req) {
        InventoryReply r = inventory.reserve(req.sku(), req.qty());  // blocks on HTTP
        return service.create(req, r.reservationId());
    }
}

// ====== 2) gRPC: typed contract, faster, still synchronous ======
// inventory.proto
service Inventory {
  rpc Reserve(ReserveRequest) returns (ReserveReply);
}

@GrpcClient("inventory")
private InventoryGrpc.InventoryBlockingStub stub;

public OrderDto place(OrderRequest req) {
    ReserveReply r = stub
        .withDeadlineAfter(500, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)               // built-in deadline
        .reserve(ReserveRequest.newBuilder()
            .setSku(req.sku()).setQty(req.qty()).build());
    return service.create(req, r.getReservationId());
}

// ====== 3) Messaging (Kafka): asynchronous, decoupled ======
@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<?> place(@RequestBody OrderRequest req) {
    Order order = service.createPending(req);
    kafkaTemplate.send("orders.placed", order.getId().toString(),
        new OrderPlaced(order.getId(), req.sku(), req.qty()));        // fire-and-forget
    return ResponseEntity.accepted().body(Map.of("id", order.getId()));
}

@KafkaListener(topics = "orders.placed", groupId = "inventory")
public void onOrder(OrderPlaced ev) {
    inventoryService.reserve(ev.sku(), ev.qty());                     // happens later
    kafkaTemplate.send("inventory.reserved",
        new InventoryReserved(ev.orderId(), UUID.randomUUID().toString()));
}
```

| Aspect | REST | gRPC | Messaging |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Coupling | temporal | temporal | none (broker buffers) |
| Latency overhead | text/JSON, HTTP/1.1 or 2 | binary protobuf, HTTP/2 multiplex | broker hop (10-50 ms) |
| Throughput | medium | high | very high |
| Schema | OpenAPI (loose) | `.proto` (strict, codegen) | Avro/JSON-Schema in registry |
| Streaming | SSE (one-way) | full bi-di streaming | native pub/sub |
| Failure mode | caller blocks | caller blocks | message stays in queue |
| Best fit | public APIs, browser | high-volume internal services | events, fan-out, async work |

- **REST** is the right default for external APIs and any boundary where curl, browser, or third-party tooling matters.
- **gRPC** wins for internal service-to-service traffic at high RPS — typed contracts catch breaking changes at compile time, HTTP/2 multiplexing avoids connection churn, deadlines and cancellation are first-class.
- **Messaging** breaks temporal coupling. Use it when the producer must not block on the consumer (events, fan-out, work distribution, audit). It introduces eventual consistency — design for it explicitly.
- A mature platform uses all three: REST at the edge, gRPC between core services, Kafka for events. Mixing is normal; pretending one fits everywhere is the anti-pattern.
